  "bodyText": "A 61-year-old fictional male patient with plaque psoriasis received his first dose of Zolapamine 75 mg. Three days later he developed a larger, more painful area of induration (approximately 8 cm) at the abdominal injection site, accompanied by low-grade fever (38.1°C) for one day. Given the fever and larger reaction size, the treating dermatologist evaluated for possible cellulitis; ultrasound of the site showed soft-tissue edema without abscess, and blood cultures were negative. The reaction was classified as a more pronounced injection-site hypersensitivity response. Zolapamine was held for one cycle, and the patient was restarted at a reduced initial dose with premedication, without recurrence. This details an adverse event associated with a drug in a specific patient.",
